#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hintdb - cleaned, deduplicated command index for `hint` (SQLite FTS5, stdlib only).

Sources (bash history, markdown/text notes, justfiles, Makefiles, git and
bash aliases; files or directories) are listed in ~/.config/hintdb/sources. Every
ingest is a full clean rebuild into a temp file that atomically replaces the
DB, because dedup is global across sources.
`find` and `query` rebuild automatically when any source file changed (mtime/size).

  hintdb add PATH... [--kind history|md|text|just|make|git|alias]
                                                     register sources, rebuild
  hintdb remove PATH...                              unregister sources, rebuild
  hintdb sources                                     list registered sources
  hintdb rebuild                                     force a clean ingest
  hintdb update                                      rebuild only if sources changed
  hintdb find [--debug] -q QUESTION [GUESS...]       the user's rows for a question
                                                     (used by hint)
  hintdb query [-n N] [--no-update] WORDS...         find's ranking for the words
                                                     alone, with scores (debugging)
  hintdb stats                                       row counts per kind and per
                                                     source file, for checking dedup
  hintdb stats --source PATH                         the entries from one source
                                                     file (or a directory's files)
  hintdb log [-n N | --all] [-c] [WORD...]           past lookups whose question or
                                                     printed commands contain all WORDs
  hintdb log --show N                                the full entry number N
  hintdb log --delete N[,N|N-M]...                   delete entries from the log
  hintdb remember [TOOL]                             the last question and the
                                                     command that answers it
                                                     (used by hint remember)
  hintdb remember --save -q QUESTION -- COMMAND      save an answer
  hintdb forget [TOOL]                               your last command (or last
                                                     TOOL command), for hint forget
  hintdb forget --where -- COMMAND                   the files and lines holding
                                                     COMMAND (nothing is changed)
  hintdb forget --save [--reason R] -- COMMAND       mark COMMAND WRONG in the
                                                     wrong file (used by hint forget)
  hintdb man -- TOOL WORDS...                        TOOL's tldr examples, man page
                                                     examples and options for WORDS
                                                     (used by hint man)
  hintdb flags -- COMMAND...                         what each flag of COMMAND does,
                                                     from help or its man page
                                                     (used by hint flags)

Query log: each `find` from the command line appends one JSON line (time,
question, guesses, printed lines, each printed row's source files) to
~/.local/share/hintdb/queries.jsonl, or HINTDB_LOG (/dev/null turns it off).

Answers: `hint remember` appends `COMMAND  # QUESTION` lines to
~/.local/share/hintdb/answers, or HINTDB_ANSWERS (/dev/null turns it off).
The file is a built-in source, read as history, whenever it exists.
`hint forget` likewise appends `COMMAND  # WRONG...` lines to
~/.local/share/hintdb/wrong, or HINTDB_WRONG.

Man pages: NAME lines of installed section 1/8 pages (from `apropos .`) go in a
separate man.db, rebuilt only when man-db's index changes; used for the summary
on model-suggested tools and listed by `query`. Set HINTDB_NO_MAN=1 to disable.

Flags: `flags` reads a bash builtin's `help`, else the man page, and prints
the option entry of each flag; HINTDB_MAN and HINTDB_HELP replace the man and
help commands (for testing).
"""
